Jesse Meyer wrote:

>Nethack has a successful development model.  That model includes not 
>releasing the source until the next version is ready.


If that were the only criterion for successful development, "the
devteam" could take 2, 5, 10 years, as long as they want, in between
releases, and smugly point out that the next version "wasn't ready
yet".  That argument has also worn very, very thin.  One of the main
thrusts of my argument is that it's taking them too damn long to be
ready, given the context of this game and the way that users play it.

*Of course* testing and bug-freeness are parts of a good development
model.  And so is communication with the user base, and so is a
release schedule that meets the needs of users.  The needs of users,
with their bug identification and their ideas and their overall
enthusiasm for growth and new challenges in the game, far outstrips
"the devteam"'s "it'll be done when it's done" attitude.

